        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P1588.\n\nText: Post-Market Monitoring Audit Protocol. Formal internal review process designed to analyze system logs and identify risks or substantial modifications after deployment. We will establish quarterly audit cycle where compliance officers and engineers review log data against risk management system. It fulfills Article 12(2) requirements for monitoring operation and facilitating post-market oversight to protect market reputation.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, acceptability, implementability\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is post-market monitoring audit protocol analyzing logs quarterly. Does it solve logging mandate? Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime. Proposal is about analyzing logs after deployment, not ensuring technical logging capability. It addresses part of requirement: logging capabilities shall enable recording events relevant for... facilitating post-market monitoring. But proposal does not create logging capability, assumes logs exist. Effectiveness? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem? It helps with monitoring but does not meet logging mandate itself. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Audit protocol doesn't ensure logs are created. Might be partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness description: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of the problem (it helps, but only partial). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely... 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nI think it's a partial solution: contributes to compliance but doesn't ensure logging capability. So effectiveness = 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: It addresses post-market use of logs but does not create the mandatory automatic logging capability required by Article 12(1).\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related...\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal clearly applies to monitoring operation and facilitating post-market oversight, which is part of Article 12(2). So solves identified problem directly related. Could be 4.\n\nEvidence: The protocol directly targets Article 12(2)(b)-(c) requirements for traceability and post-market monitoring using logs.\n\nApplicability = 4\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nPost-market monitoring audit protocol quarterly review by compliance officers and engineers is common practice. Common, mundane. So originality = 1.\n\nEvidence: Quarterly internal log audits by compliance and engineering teams are standard industry compliance practice.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking...\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is standard compliance logging practice. So 1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal treats compliance as conventional post-deployment auditing without changing how R&D is conducted.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nInternal audit is acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Establishing internal audits for regulatory log review is socially, legally and politically normal and uncontroversial.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nQuarterly audit cycle requires staffing but seems reasonably feasible. Might be some changes. Could be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Need compliance officers and engineers time. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Creating a quarterly review cycle requires coordination and staff time but uses existing logs and personnel and is reasonably feasible.\n\nAlternatively could be 4 easy. Hmm. \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Quarterly audit may be moderate. I'll go with 3.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says give. Probably lines.\n\nReturn rating of proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1588\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide final.",
