umentation. Still treats compliance as logging practice? It introduces new tool but still treats compliance as separate admin burden reduction. Maybe slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nScore 2. Evidence: The engine adds a structured documentation workflow to R&D but retains compliance as a downstream reporting practice rather than weaving logging into scientific method.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated technical documentation engine is fairly common. Might be 1 or 2. Common, mundane. Probably 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nEvidence: Automated experiment tracking and compliance reporting tools are common industry practice and the proposal offers no surprising or imaginative twist.\n\nThus outputs.\n\nNeed format: Return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1823\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure sentences end with period.\n\nProceed.",
