. That's maybe not directly solving logging mandate? Might be less implementable quickly. I'd say 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4=paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3=paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2=slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1=paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposing open standard positions lab as scientific authority on governance. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It creates new offering/authority. Could be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship, new partners born from compliance. Maybe 3. Not breaking paradigm entirely? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Might be 3. I'd pick 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nOpen standard for high-risk traceability is unusual? Might be interesting. Collaboration to draft schema balancing transparency with IP protection. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not necessarily never expressed before. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not