al standard-setting. So applicability is low: solves unrelated problem? Actually positioning as scientific authority is unrelated to meeting logging mandate. Might be 2.\n\nLet's reconsider: Stated problem every proposal addresses: an AI R&D lab subject to this regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is about creating open standard via collaboration. Does that meet logging mandate? No. It adapts R&D towards governance authority. Could be considered adapting R&D accordingly. Might be applicable.\n\nI think acceptability 4, implementability 2, paradigm_relatedness 3, originality 3, applicability 3, effectiveness 2? Effectiveness: will it solve problem? If you could do it, would it solve main problem of meeting logging mandate? No. It would not ensure logs recorded per Article12(1-3). So maybe effectiveness 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nThe definition: effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Would standard solve logging mandate? No. So not 4. 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Could contribute by providing format. Maybe contributes.\n\nI think choose effectiveness 3.\n\nLet's output in required format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON.\n\nProvide lines:\nP1945\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nLet's produce final answer.",
