ural persons? Might be partial. But overall reasonable and will solve main problem? Probably contributes? Hmm.\n\nEffectiveness 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does middleware capture all required logs? It mentions track usage timestamps, input data matches, database references. Article 12(3) also needs identification of natural persons involved in verification. Not mentioned. Also need logging events relevant for risk situations etc. Might be partial. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial.\n\nI think effectiveness 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is standardized software layer plug-and-play API. That's introducing new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified compliance logging middleware is fairly common idea. Might be common, mundane. Could be 2? Interesting? I'd say common practice, maybe 1 = common mundane boring. Standard middleware for compliance is common. Probably 1.\n\nLet's output in order: acceptability_evidence sentence then acceptability, e