aluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expect unified logging framework is expected. So common, mundane. Level 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nLevel 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\nLevel 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\nThe proposal introduces standardized library, plug-and-play module, centralized database. That's new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? Or paradigm preserving? It says eliminates redundant development effort across different research teams and optimizes storage resource allocation. That is introducing new tools/processes but compliance remains logging practice.\n\nDefinition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis introduces new elements tools. Might be 2.\n\nProbably paradigm_relatedness =2.\n\nLet's output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably plain lines.\n\nInstruction: Return the rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (an integer level...)\n\nProbably output like:\n\nid: P2815\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nCheck applicability evidence: The proposal builds a common API library to automatically capture timestamps, input r