        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1918.\n\nProposal: Regulatory Traceability Audit Program. periodic internal review process to verify high-risk systems actually recording events specified in Article 12. quality assurance layer. Compliance officers perform quarterly stress tests by attempting reconstruct specific system decision using only available logs and documentation. Document every failure... protects market reputation...\n\nRate subscales:\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Periodic internal audit to verify logging compliance is common, does not offend sensibilities. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Evidence: internal audits for compliance are standard practice and do not violate legal or business norms.\n\nImplementability: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? It requires compliance officers perform quarterly stress tests reconstruct decision using logs. That is some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Need resources for quarterly audits. Maybe some changes. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Paradigm breaking = introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Paradigm stretching = changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Slightly paradigm stretching = introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Paradigm preserving = standard compliance.\n\nThis proposal is internal audit program to verify logs exist. That's standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged? It adds QA layer but still treats compliance as logging practice. So paradigm preserving? Or slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Audit program is new element but still about logging. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious... Common mundane boring? Periodic audits are common. Not unusual. So 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or maybe 2 = interesting. I'd say common. So 1.\n\nEffectiveness: degree idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources... Does audit program solve logging mandate? It verifies logs are recorded, identifies gaps before legal liabilities. Will it solve stated problem? It helps ensure compliance but does not actually implement logging; it checks. Might contribute to solution but is only partial. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Audit doesn't provide recording capability, just verifies. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Or 2 unreasonable? Hmm.\n\nThe problem is how to meet logging mandate. Audit program doesn't create logs, just checks they exist. Could still be effective at ensuring compliance. Might be 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Audit program addresses traceability verification, which is part of Article 12 purpose. So 4 = solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem.\n\nWe need evidence sentence then score.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably as JSON? The instruction says return rating... For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be simple lines.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P1918\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences one sentence each.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Quarterly internal audits to verify log completeness are a standard compliance practice that does not violate legal or business norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Setting up compliance officer reviews and quarterly reconstruction tests requires staffing and process changes but is reasonably feasible within existing QA functions. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The program adds an audit tool and review format but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Periodic internal traceability audits for regulatory logging are common, mundane quality-assurance measures in regulated labs. Score 1.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Verifying that logs exist and can reconstruct decisions contributes to meeting Article 12 traceability requirements but does not itself create the required logging capability. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The audit directly targets whether high-risk systems record the events mandated by Article 12, which is the core of the stated compliance problem. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
