        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nNeed rating for proposal P2361.\n\nProposal: Automated Provenance Research Initiative. Dedicated R&D project to develop AI-driven methods for automatically generating technical documentation and traceability reports from raw system logs. Researchers will explore using specialized language models to synthesize comprehensive compliance narratives based on version control history and experiment trackers. Transforms regulatory burden into high-impact scientific contribution that preserves institutional ambition.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nacceptability evidence: ... degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies...\nThe proposal is R&D project to automate documentation. Acceptable? Likely socially acceptable, doesn't violate norms. Probably 4? It is common strategies? Developing AI to generate docs might be okay. No violation. Could be somewhat uncommon? Might be 3? Common strategies that violate no norms: 4 = common strategies... Automated provenance research seems not totally common but not offensive. Possibly 4? Acceptability: it's an internal R&D to comply, no sensibility offended. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping AI-driven methods for automatically generating technical documentation from logs is a dedicated R&D project. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Not easy low cost. Probably 2? Might be 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Hmm dedicated R&D project to develop AI-driven methods... that's research, uncertain, costly. I'd rate 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated generation of compliance docs from logs using language models is somewhat unusual but not super novel? Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Maybe 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal transforms regulatory burden into high-impact scientific contribution that preserves institutional ambition. That suggests changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Might be paradigm stretching? It says transforms burden into scientific contribution. That's changing relationship. Could be 3 or 4. Paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship: compliance becomes research. Might be paradigm breaking? It introduces AI-driven methods for generating docs, that's new element, and changes relationship. Could be 4. Hmm anchor: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This fits. Maybe 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable unlikely to solve\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nProblem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is to develop methods to automatically generate technical documentation and traceability reports from raw logs. That would help meet logging mandate? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs, and availability of technical documentation. Automating generation of documentation from logs would address documentation burden, but does it ensure logs are recorded per Article 12? It generates reports from logs, assumes logs exist. Might contribute partially. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Could it solve fully? It doesn't guarantee logging capabilities for risk situations etc. It's about documentation generation, not logging itself. So partial. Score 3.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = ...\n\nProposal clearly applies to meeting logging mandate via automated docs. That's directly related. Maybe 4? It addresses compliance narratives based on version control and experiment trackers. Article 12 requires logs and technical documentation. Generating documentation from logs is directly related. So applicability 4.\n\nWe need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: acceptability, implementability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide sentences.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nacceptability_evidence: The proposal pursues internal R&D to automate compliance documentation using AI models and does not violate laws or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Developing specialized language models to synthesize compliance narratives from logs requires a dedicated research project with significant cost and technical risk. Score 2.\n\noriginality_evidence: Using AI-driven synthesis of regulatory narratives from version control and experiment trackers is unusual and shows imagination beyond standard logging tools. Score 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The initiative reframes compliance logging as a scientific research agenda, changing the relationship between the lab's R&D and its compliance obligations. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Automating documentation generation from existing logs would ease traceability reporting but does not by itself guarantee the mandated automatic event logging for high-risk AI systems. Score 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: Generating technical documentation and traceability reports directly addresses the Article 12 requirement for keeping records and making technical documentation available throughout system lifetime. Score 4.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably just list. Might be key-value.\n\nLet's output as simple lines.\n\n",
