        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2405: Unified Compliance Logging Framework. Standardized software library integrated into all internal AI pipelines to automate mandatory event recording...\n\nWe need rate six subscales in order:\n\nparadigm_relatedness\noriginality\neffectiveness\napplicability\nacceptability\nimplementability\n\nNeed one sentence evidence then score.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal is standardized software library integrated into pipelines to automate mandatory event recording. This introduces new element tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Does it change relationship? It reduces overhead but still logging practice. Likely slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified logging framework is common. Probably common, mundane. Might be interesting? Standard software library for compliance logging is common practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nWill automate mandatory event recording, capture timestamps, input references, user identifiers. Addresses Article 12 requirements: logging events, period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Seems would solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem? Might be partial because specific requirements for point 1(a) Annex III etc. But framework provides consistent interface. Likely will solve main problem. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nIt directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates\n\nStandardized library is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDevelop middleware layer integrated into all internal AI pipelines. That's significant change across lab, maybe expensive. Could be some changes reasonably feasible? Integrating into all pipelines is significant. Might be 2? Let's think: standardized software library plug-and-play module for any researcher starting high-risk project. Reduces per-project engineering overhead. Implementation requires developing middleware and integrating into pipelines – that's significant change or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. The anchor: 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible.\n\nI think it's significant change across lab. Might be 2. Could argue 3. I'd go with 3? Let's parse: \"develop a middleware layer that automatically captures timestamps...\" That is non-radical? Probably requires engineering effort. Hmm.\n\nGiven scarce implementation resources, committing to develop unified library for all pipelines is not trivial. Probably significant change. I'll give 2.\n\nNeed format: return rating echoing id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON output.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P2405\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's produce concise one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure order listed.\n\nLet's output.",
