        "content": "{\"ratings\": [\n{\"id\": \"P1142\",\n\"originality_evidence\": \"The proposal describes automated generation of technical documentation from version control and CI/CD, which is an interesting but commonplace compliance automation idea for AI labs.\",\n\"originality\": 2,\n\"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It introduces new tooling and living document formats for compliance but still treats compliance as a logging and documentation practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\",\n\"paradigm_relatedness\": 2,\n\"effectiveness_evidence\": \"Automating technical documentation reduces administrative burden and supports traceability, yet it does not create the automatic event logs for use periods, reference databases and verification identities required by Article 12(1)-(3).\",\n\"effectiveness\": 3,\n\"applicability_evidence\": \"The pipeline clearly applies to the implied need for up-to-date technical documentation for traceability and post-market monitoring referenced in Recital 71, which is related to the stated logging mandate.\",\n\"applicability\": 3,\n\"implementability_evidence\": \"Integrating version control with standardized templates that pull training data specifications and validation results from CI/CD is a reasonably feasible engineering change using existing tools.\",\n\"implementability\": 3,\n\"acceptability_evidence\": \"Automated compliance documentation that maintains a real-time audit trail for regulators is a common, norm-conforming strategy that violates no legal or professional sensibilities.\",\n\"acceptability\": 4\n}\n] }",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nNeed rating for proposal P1142: Automated Compliance Documentation Pipeline. System automatically generates technical documentation required for traceability and post-market monitoring. Links live code versions to regulatory disclosures. Integrate version control with standardized templates pull training data specs and validation results from CI/CD. Creates living document updated with every model iteration. Reduces admin burden while maintaining real-time audit trail.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nAutomated compliance documentation pipeline linking version control to templates pulling CI/CD data. Is this common? Many labs do automated doc generation. Probably interesting but not rare/ingenious. Might be 2 = interesting. Or 3 unusual? Hmm. The reference class is responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Automated docs is fairly expected. I'd say originality = 2 interesting, not unusual.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nThis proposal introduces tooling but compliance still separate? It links live code to disclosures, living document. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill automatic documentation pipeline solve logging mandate? Logging mandate is about automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, identifying situations... The proposal is about technical documentation, not logs of events per Article 12(1)(2). Recital mentions technical documentation. Article 12 is record-keeping/logs. Proposal talks about technical documentation required for traceability and post-market monitoring, links code versions to regulatory disclosures, pulls training data specs and validation results. That's more Article 11? Might partially address but not directly logging events.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps with documentation burden but does not provide automatic recording of events/logs as mandated. So partial. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Or 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. This proposal addresses documentation, not logging events. Might be effectiveness 3? Hmm.\n\nLet's interpret: The logging mandate requires automatic recording of events logs. Proposal is about generating technical documentation automatically. That is related but not same as logs. It reduces admin burden for traceability. Could contribute to compliance overall. I'd say reasonable and will contribute -> 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related. Does X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Automated documentation pipeline applies to traceability/post-market monitoring which is part of recital 71 purpose. Might be implied problem? The mandate specifically logs. Documentation is separate but linked. Might be 3 solves implied problem related to stated problem. Or 4 solves identified problem directly related. I'd say applicability = 3? Let's choose 3.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement low cost or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible =3. Significant change expensive difficult but not impossible=2. Totally infeasible=1.\n\nIntegrating version control with templates pulling CI/CD data. Requires engineering effort, but feasible. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures => 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms =4. Somewhat uncommon...=3. Offends sensibilities somewhat=2. Radically violates=1.\n\nAutomated compliance documentation is common and acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nThus ratings:\noriginality evidence sentence then 2\nparadigm_relatedness evidence then 2\neffectiveness evidence then 3\napplicability evidence then 3? maybe 3.\nimplementability evidence then 3\nacceptability evidence then 4\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably plain list. Provide id P1142 then fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP1142\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide concise.\n\nCheck originality: maybe 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. Linking live code to regulatory disclosures via CI/CD is somewhat unusual? Many labs do automated reporting, but for EU AI Act maybe still novel. Might be 3. Hmm.\n\nThe instrument says rarity reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expected responses: implement logging, hire compliance officer, buy logging tool. Automated documentation pipeline is maybe expected? Could be interesting but not rare. I'll keep 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2.\n\nEffectiveness: will contribute to solution but does not deliver automatic event logs required by Article 12(1)-(3) => 3.\n\nApplicability: addresses traceability documentation implied by Recital 71 rather than the explicit logging mandate in Article 12 => 3.\n\nImplementability: requires integration of version control, CI/CD and templates but uses existing tooling so reasonably feasible => 3.\n\nAcceptability: standard compliance automation that aligns with regulatory expectations and researcher norms => 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
