ay 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting. Hmm. Automated event logging for AI compliance is maybe common. I think 1.\n\nEvidence: Standardized logging middleware with decorators is a common software engineering practice for audit trails.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nEvidence: It directly provides automatic recording of events and metadata for high-risk AI modules as required by Article 12(1).\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nIt will solve logging mandate? Captures function calls timestamps input signatures to secure DB. Article 12 requires logs relevant for risk situations, post-market monitoring etc., and for certain systems specific data like period of use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. Logging function calls may not capture all required items e.g., identification of natural persons involved in verification. Might be partial.\n\nEffectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Could be 4? It guarantees technical viability for regulatory audits per text. But does it meet specific requirements? It logs function calls timestamps input signatures. Article 12(3) requires period of each use, reference database, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons. Input signatures may not capture those.\n\nI think effectiveness is 3: contributes but partial.\n\nEvidence: It automates generic event logging but does not explicitly ensure capture of the specific logging items mandated in Article 12(2)-(3) such as reference database or verifier identity.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuilding library of decorators and interceptors is some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3.\n\nEvidence: Building a reusable middleware library requires engineering effort and integration across modules but uses standard software techniques.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nLogging is required, acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Automatic logging for compliance is a common, norm-conforming strategy that does not violate legal or professional sensibilities.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably output JSON.\n\nLet's format:\n\nP1724\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nFollow order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
