ce: It creates a shared service and budget carve-out for logging without redefining R&D goals.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nDedicated compliance fund is common practice. Probably common, mundane. So 1.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nBudget carve-out and personnel pool is reasonably feasible. Some changes. Could be 3. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Evidence: Establishing a shared service team requires organizational change but uses existing budgeting mechanisms.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates...\n\nBudget carve-out is acceptable. Common. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. Proposal allocates resources to shared service to handle logging, prevents draining grants. Will it solve logging mandate? It provides resources but doesn't specify technical logging solution. It contributes to meeting mandate by ensuring funding. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Does not ensure logs actually meet Article 12 requirements. So partial.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt clearly applies to resource alloca