ric proposal may be assumed sufficient.\n\nI'll pick 4? Safer to say contributes but not fully ensures compliance with all Article 12(3) specifics. The proposal mentions only inputs outputs timestamps, not reference DB or verification persons. So partial.\n\nThus effectiveness = 3.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Middleware logging is standard compliance, acceptable. No violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Sidecar pattern / decorator-based logging layer integrated into common ML libraries. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivially easy? Might require integration across projects. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Probably not easy at low cost for all high-risk projects. Decouples regulatory... Implementability maybe 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious. 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. Automated telemetry middleware with sidecar pattern for AI logging is common practice; not rare. Might be interesting but common. So originality = 1 = common, mundane, boring? Or 2 = interesting? Standard logging middleware is common in ML ops. Probably 1 or 2. The reference class is responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expect sidecar/decorator logging is expected. So common, mundane. Originality = 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools formats processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nProposal decouples regulatory requirements from research experimentation to protect prototyping velocity. That suggests treating compliance as separate layer, not changing R&D. It introduces new elements (middleware) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nAlternatively paradigm preserving = standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. But they explicitly decouple to protect velocity, which is a slight change. I think 2.\n\nThus scores:\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\noriginality 1\nparadigm_relatedness 2\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id. Likely output lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2652\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise evidence sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The middleware directly provides automatic capture of inputs, outputs and timestamps across high-risk projects to satisfy Article 12's logging mandate.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It would generate core logs needed for traceability but does not explicitly guarantee capture of Annex III-specific items such as reference database or verifier identity.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automatic telemetry via standard API wrappers is a common compliance practice that violates no legal, social or business norms.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating a sidecar or decorator layer into