elationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis proposal introduces formal internal protocols, mandatory templates, peer-review checkpoints. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be paradigm preserving? It says establishes new institutional standard. Could be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nI think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nEvidence: It adds templates and checkpoints to R&D workflow but keeps compliance framed as documentation rather than redefining research-compliance relationship.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is internal documentation, peer review. Acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Formal documentation and peer review for high-risk AI are legally encouraged and socially uncontroversial.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult\nProposal requires mandatory templates, peer-review checkpoints at each stage linked to version control. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Implementing templates and checkpoints requires process change and staff training but uses existing version control infrastructure.\n\nScore 3.\n\nNow output format: For each subscale give <subscal