roblem. Stated problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposes library to capture timestamps, input hashes, user identifiers by default. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use etc. Capturing timestamps and identifiers applies. So applicability 4? It solves identified problem directly related. Yes.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Will this solve logging mandate? It captures timestamps, input hashes, user identifiers by default. Article 12 also requires specific items for point 1(a) Annex III: period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... This proposal may partially cover but not all? It captures timestamps, input hashes, user identifiers. Might be partial. Could still be reasonable and will contribute. Probably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial). Because logs need specific events relevant for risk etc. It captures generic. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Developing shared internal library is some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not trivially easy; need software engineering team develop library, integrate across models. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Logging compliance is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNow format output: echo id P1377. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a shared logging library and schemas but keeps compliance as separate logging practice without changing R&D-compliance relationship.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\noriginality_evidence: A standardized internal logging library for regulatory compliance is a common, mundane engineering response expected from labs facing logging mandates.\noriginality: 1\n\napplicability_evidence: It directly addresses the mandate to technically allow automatic event recording across high-risk AI models to meet Article 12.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: While it enables consistent timestamp and identifier logging, it does not guarantee capture of all Article 12 specific events like risk situations or Annex III data required for full compliance.\neffectiveness: 3\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a shared library requires software engineering effort and integration across diverse projects but is reasonably feasible with internal resources.\nimplementability: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Standardized logging for regulatory compliance follows legal requirements and does not offend social, legal or business norms.\nacceptability: 4\n\nReturn JSON? Probably plain. Instruction says return rating echoing id. Provide each.\n\nWill output as list.\n\n",
